# {{ project_name }}

> One line saying what this is.

## Getting started

```bash
git clone <this repository>
cd {{ project_name }}
uv sync --locked   # 1. dependencies, the same command CI runs; the lockfile is used as is
uv run pytest      # 2. tests
```

## Developing

```bash
uv run ruff check . && uv run ruff format .   # lint and format
uv run mypy .                                  # types
uv run pytest                                  # tests
uv build                                       # build
```

{% if host == 'gitlab' %}The pipeline runs each of these as a separate job, plus a secret scan, a
dependency review and the merge request checks. Pass them locally first.{% else %}CI runs each of these as a separate check, plus a secret scan and CodeQL.
Pass them locally first. The list of required checks is the repository's
ruleset, so it is not repeated here.{% endif %}

{% if archetype in ['backend', 'data-ml', 'web'] %}## Configuration

Copy [`.env.example`](.env.example) to `.env` and fill in the values.
**`.env` is never committed.**
{%- if archetype == 'web' %} Nothing reads `.env` by itself: run with
`uv run --env-file .env python -m {{ package_name }}`, or
`uv run --env-file .env python manage.py <command>`.

On a new database, apply the migrations before the first start:
`uv run --env-file .env python manage.py migrate`. `/healthz` does not touch
the database, so it answers on an unmigrated one while log-in and the admin
fail.

HSTS preload and include-subdomains are off, and their two deployment
warnings are silenced in `settings.py` with the reason: preload is hard to
undo, and subdomains reach the whole domain. Both are set in the production
block of `settings.py`, and each comes off `SILENCED_SYSTEM_CHECKS` when set:

- Include subdomains: `SECURE_HSTS_INCLUDE_SUBDOMAINS = True`, once every
  subdomain answers over HTTPS.
- Preload needs all of: include subdomains as above,
  `SECURE_HSTS_SECONDS = 31536000` (one year) or more, and
  `SECURE_HSTS_PRELOAD = True`. Then submit the domain at
  <https://hstspreload.org>; browsers apply it only after that, and removal
  takes months.

{% if hosting == 'render' -%}
To deploy, create the service and its database once from `render.yaml` in
Render's dashboard (New > Blueprint, this repository). Then, in the
Blueprint's Settings, set Auto Sync to No. Render deploys each commit to the
default branch after its checks pass, and runs the migrations first. A change
to `render.yaml` itself is the exception: with Auto Sync on, Render applies it
on push, before any check. With it off, press Manual Sync once that change is
merged and its checks have passed.

- **The address.** The form asks for `DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS` before the service
  exists. Type the `onrender.com` name you expect, or your own domain if you
  have one. Either way the service answers on the address Render actually
  gives it, which can have a few extra characters (`<name>-ab12.onrender.com`).
- **The first deploy waits for the database.** Render creates the database and
  the service together, and the database can take a minute or two to start.
  The step before each deploy waits up to two minutes for it, then applies the
  migrations.
- **The first account.** Nobody can log in until you make an account. Open the
  web service's own page in Render (not the Blueprint's), choose **Shell**, and
  run `python -m django createsuperuser`. The Shell does not show the password
  as you type it, so write it somewhere first and paste it, both times it is
  asked and later at the log-in page. Typed blind, it can come out different
  from what you meant.
- **Getting data back.** On the database's page in Render, open **Recovery**
  and choose **Restore Database**. Pick a time at least ten minutes ago: Render
  makes a new database as it was then, and the old one stays as it is. Then,
  on the web service's **Environment** page, set `DATABASE_URL` to the new
  database's Internal Database URL (on the new database's page) and save it,
  so the service is deployed again on the new database. How far back you can go depends on the workspace
  plan: the past 3 days on Hobby, the past 7 days on Pro or higher. Render's
  backups page (<https://render.com/docs/postgresql-backups>, read 2026-10-24)
  also says: "You can't restore to a time that's within ten minutes of the
  current time."
